r was real, and that was enough.\n\nShe came back at midnight — the client, or the woman behind the client. Her story changed the way stories do when the truth puts on its coat. a bloodstained matchbook, she admitted, was stolen. From the corrupt councilman."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "The dame vanished. Of course she did. Hank Mercer found her scarf in the office, red as a warning, and underneath it a note: 'Forgive me. They have my brother.' So that was the price. Everyone has one.\n\nthe corrupt councilman squeezed back. First the office: tossed, the safe cracked, a bloodstained matchbook's copy gone — but Hank Mercer keeps the originals where no one looks. Then Lou the bartender: a warning delivered with fists in an alley off Grand Central Station.\n\nHank Mercer sat up all night with a bloodstained matchbook and the photographs and the whiskey going untouched. By dawn there was a plan. It wasn't a good plan. It was the only plan."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "The dame got her brother back and Hank Mercer got the rest of the fee, which was more than the insulting original. She cried. Hank Mercer didn't buy the tears but didn't charge for the shoulder, either.\n\nHank Mercer walked into the corrupt councilman's office at noon, when Grand Central Station was bright and honest-looking, which made the lies inside seem darker. a bloodstained matchbook went on the desk. The photographs went on the desk. The truth went on the desk.\n\n'You're finished,' Hank Mercer said. the corrupt councilman smiled the smile of someone who has bought judges. 'Am I?' Then Lou the bartender opened the door with the district attorney, and Ruby, and the dame's brother, alive, and the smile died."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "Grand Central Station kept being Grand Central Station: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Hank Mercer could live with that. Hank Mercer had lived with worse.\n